    "id": 3104,
    "title": "AI and Software Systems Engineering in 2026: Talent and Mental Potential",
    "date": "2026-07-03T19:06:33+00:00",
    "category": "هوش مصنوعی و مدل‌های زبانی",
    "word_count": 451,
    "views": "26",
    "shares": 14,
    "full_text": "AI and Software Systems Engineering in 2026: Talent and Mental Potential\nEngineering in the new era has become a strategic mental sport. This new career path is highly \"talent-driven\". If your mind has the capacity to simultaneously manage multiple agents, pass their outputs through a logic filter, and connect the puzzles with high energy, you are indeed at the forefront of this technology and will leave behind many individuals with years of traditional experience but static minds.\nSystem Architects and Engineers (Pre-AI Era)\nSystem architecture is one of the oldest and most senior roles in software engineering (since the 1970s and 80s).\nWhat was their job? A system architect was someone who, instead of getting bogged down with lines of code, looked at the \"big picture\". They decided how the database should be designed, how servers should communicate with each other, what the data flow should be, and how system security should be ensured.\nThe difference today: In the past, the system architect designed these blueprints (like UML diagrams) and then delegated their implementation to a team of human programmers (namely frontend, backend, and database developers). Today, an AI-proficient system architect designs those same blueprints but delegates the execution of a large part of it to artificial intelligence agents.\nThe Concept of an \"Agent\" in the Past\nThe concept of an \"Intelligent Agent\" is a classic concept in computer science that existed long before\nChatGPT\nand Large Language Models (LLMs).\nWe had systems similar to agents in the past as well, but with a completely different structure:\nRule-based Agents: In the past, agents were \"deterministic\" software robots. This means the engineer had to write all the instructions for them in the form of (If-Then-Else). Web crawlers used by Google to index sites, or trading bots in the stock market, are classic examples of agents. They were automated, but they lacked the ability to \"understand\" or \"infer\".\nAgents in Game Development (NPCs): In game engines, enemies or non-playable characters (NPCs) were a type of agent that made decisions using Finite State Machines or Behavior Trees.\nMicroservices: In software architecture, large applications were divided into small, independent services where each had a specific task and communicated with one another via APIs. This network of microservices is very similar to a network of modern-day agents.\nParadigm Shift: From \"Deterministic Logic\" to \"Probabilistic Reasoning\"\nWhat has changed in recent years (and especially in 2026) is these agents becoming genuinely intelligent.\nIn the past, if a software agent encountered an error it wasn't programmed for, it would stop (Crash). However, modern agents (which are connected to LLM engines) possess reasoning capabilities. They can read code, analyze errors, suggest new solutions, and even change their execution path.",
